{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-10435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}=9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063772/state.json" "file_sha256" "cabb9dce3c706f2d069f1376fc989fb008f9b83c8f1c9fdbf703c3c0a407da1f"} {"question" "q-10435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, we divide both sides of the equation by $9$ and then simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{9}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $a=\\sqrt{9}=3$ and $b=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{1}x \\\\  & =\\pm 3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063772/state.json" "file_sha256" "cabb9dce3c706f2d069f1376fc989fb008f9b83c8f1c9fdbf703c3c0a407da1f"} {"question" "q-10435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063772/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3ed0db2ee72af1d9cc1a7e87d55ff89b499ad9ec994545431b201b2611aa4988"} {"question" "q-10435" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14063772/state.json" "file_sha256" "cabb9dce3c706f2d069f1